WEBVTT

00:00.416 --> 00:01.533
- [Voiceover] Let's see if we can find

00:03.394 --> 00:07.200
the Maclaurin series
representation of f of x,

00:07.200 --> 00:09.633
where f of x is equal to x to the third

00:09.966 --> 00:12.767
times cosine of x squared.

00:12.767 --> 00:14.993
I encourage you to pause the
video and now try to do it.

00:14.993 --> 00:16.500
Remember, the Maclaurin series is just

00:16.500 --> 00:19.700
the Taylor series centered at zero.

00:19.933 --> 00:23.933
Let's say our goal here is
the first five non-zero terms

00:24.333 --> 00:26.635
of the Maclaurin series representation,

00:26.635 --> 00:28.600
or Maclaurin series approximation of this.

00:29.967 --> 00:31.900
I'm assuming you had paused the video

00:31.900 --> 00:33.533
and you had attempted to do this.

00:33.600 --> 00:35.267
There's a good chance
that you might have gotten

00:35.267 --> 00:36.833
quite frustrated when you did this,

00:36.833 --> 00:39.702
because in order to find a
Taylor series, Maclaurin series,

00:39.933 --> 00:42.367
we need to find the
derivatives of this function,

00:42.367 --> 00:43.566
and as soon as you start to do that,

00:43.566 --> 00:44.833
it starts to get painful.

00:45.134 --> 00:47.400
F prime of x is going to be,

00:47.400 --> 00:48.635
let's see, product rule,

00:48.635 --> 00:52.800
so it's three x squared
times cosine of x squared

00:53.335 --> 00:55.366
plus x to the third

00:56.000 --> 00:57.833
times the derivative of this thing,

00:57.838 --> 01:02.266
which is going to be two x
times negative sine of x,

01:02.533 --> 01:05.200
negative sine of x squared.

01:05.300 --> 01:06.566
Just that was be pretty painful.

01:06.566 --> 01:07.938
But it's only going to
get more and more painful

01:07.938 --> 01:10.559
as we take the second and
third and fourth derivative.

01:10.559 --> 01:11.500
We might have to take more,

01:11.500 --> 01:13.766
because some of these terms
might end up being zeros.

01:14.233 --> 01:16.066
We want the first five non-zero terms.

01:16.066 --> 01:19.466
The second derivative, this
is going to be painful.

01:20.334 --> 01:22.003
This is going to be painful.

01:22.003 --> 01:23.400
Then the third and fourth derivatives

01:23.400 --> 01:25.266
are going to be even more painful.

01:25.900 --> 01:26.867
So what do we do here?

01:26.867 --> 01:27.833
You could just do this.

01:27.833 --> 01:29.334
Let's evaluate them each at zero,

01:29.334 --> 01:31.900
and then use those for our coefficients,

01:31.900 --> 01:33.935
but you're probably guessing correctly

01:34.088 --> 01:36.266
that there's an easier way to do this.

01:37.166 --> 01:38.633
I will give you a hint.

01:40.733 --> 01:45.600
We know what the Maclaurin
series for cosine of x is.

01:45.866 --> 01:47.200
We've done that in the previous video.

01:47.200 --> 01:50.261
If you want to see that
again, there's another video.

01:50.266 --> 01:53.733
Look up "cosine Taylor series
at zero" on Khan Academy,

01:53.733 --> 01:54.966
and you'll find that.

01:54.966 --> 01:55.966
But we already know from that,

01:55.966 --> 01:59.566
and this is one of the most
famous Maclaurin series,

01:59.566 --> 02:01.866
we know that this is a g of x.

02:01.866 --> 02:03.200
We say g of x

02:04.200 --> 02:06.101
is equal to cosine of x.

02:07.566 --> 02:09.800
Well, we know how this, what this is like.

02:10.650 --> 02:13.301
There are the Maclaurin
series approximation of that

02:13.301 --> 02:16.700
is going to be one minus x squared

02:16.933 --> 02:18.666
over two factorial,

02:18.803 --> 02:23.400
plus x to the fourth over four factorial,

02:24.166 --> 02:28.566
minus x to the sixth over six factorial,

02:28.933 --> 02:30.900
plus, and I could keep
going on and on and on.

02:30.900 --> 02:32.200
You kind of see where this is going.

02:32.205 --> 02:35.933
Plus x to the eighth over eight factorial,

02:36.533 --> 02:37.633
and it just keeps going,

02:37.633 --> 02:39.619
minus, plus, on and on and on and on.

02:40.033 --> 02:42.967
But we wanted first five
terms, so this is a start.

02:42.967 --> 02:45.044
I know we wanted the first
five terms of this thing,

02:45.044 --> 02:45.733
but bear with me.

02:45.733 --> 02:47.100
We'll see how this thing right over here

02:47.100 --> 02:48.407
is going to be useful.

02:48.500 --> 02:50.433
Now that I've given you a
little bit of a reminder

02:50.433 --> 02:53.633
on the Maclaurin series
representation of cosine of x,

02:54.101 --> 02:56.666
my hint to you is, can we use this

02:57.267 --> 03:01.067
to find the Maclaurin series
representation of this,

03:01.233 --> 03:02.433
right up here?

03:02.433 --> 03:06.405
Remember, all this is, this
is x to the third times ...

03:06.405 --> 03:07.933
And I'm just rereading it for you,

03:07.933 --> 03:12.400
this is x to the third
times g of x squared.

03:13.219 --> 03:15.833
That's a sizable hint.

03:16.366 --> 03:17.733
I encourage you to pause the video again

03:17.733 --> 03:19.633
and see if you can work through this.

03:21.100 --> 03:22.266
Let me rewrite.

03:22.266 --> 03:23.833
I'm assuming you had a go at it.

03:23.833 --> 03:25.568
Let me rewrite what I just wrote.

03:25.568 --> 03:28.800
I just told you that g of x ...

03:29.300 --> 03:32.367
or f of x. F of x ...

03:33.012 --> 03:34.266
If I wanted to write it as,

03:35.927 --> 03:37.656
I guess you could say as a function,

03:37.656 --> 03:40.533
or if I want to construct it using g of x,

03:40.533 --> 03:44.159
I could rewrite it as x
to the third times ...

03:44.159 --> 03:45.367
instead of cosine of x squared,

03:45.367 --> 03:47.300
that's the same thing as g of x squared.

03:47.366 --> 03:50.133
So x to the third times g,

03:51.034 --> 03:53.000
g of x squared.

03:53.833 --> 03:57.566
G of x squared.

03:58.867 --> 04:00.066
G of x is just cosine.

04:00.066 --> 04:02.134
G of x squared is going
to be cosine of x squared.

04:02.466 --> 04:04.500
Then you're going to multiply
that times x to the third.

04:05.534 --> 04:09.900
Well, can't we just apply this
insight to the approximation?

04:10.502 --> 04:11.866
You might be asking that question.

04:11.933 --> 04:14.900
My answer to you is,
"Yes, you absolutely can."

04:15.300 --> 04:18.566
Notice, when you substitute
your xes for x squared,

04:18.566 --> 04:20.066
you'll just get another polynomial.

04:20.066 --> 04:22.000
And if you multiply
that by x to the third,

04:22.000 --> 04:23.634
you're just going to
get another polynomial.

04:23.634 --> 04:27.766
That actually will be the
Maclaurin series representation

04:27.766 --> 04:29.734
of what we started off with.

04:29.734 --> 04:32.233
We actually will get the
Maclaurin series representation

04:32.233 --> 04:34.333
of this thing right over here.

04:34.333 --> 04:36.466
So we could say that f of x

04:38.000 --> 04:39.800
is approximately equal to

04:40.433 --> 04:42.700
x to the third times

04:43.366 --> 04:44.700
x to the third times ...

04:44.700 --> 04:46.667
Let me get myself some
space right over here.

04:47.304 --> 04:49.500
G of x squared.

04:49.804 --> 04:52.400
Over here, this is
approximation for g of x,

04:52.400 --> 04:53.873
and if we kept going on and on forever,

04:53.873 --> 04:57.000
it would be a representation of g of x.

04:57.233 --> 04:59.003
So every place where we see an x,

04:59.003 --> 05:00.433
let's replace it with an x squared.

05:00.533 --> 05:02.967
This is going to be one minus ...

05:03.400 --> 05:05.567
So x squared squared is x to the fourth,

05:06.401 --> 05:08.133
x to the fourth over two factorial,

05:08.133 --> 05:08.873
which is really just two,

05:08.873 --> 05:10.867
but I like to put the factorial there

05:10.867 --> 05:12.033
because you see the pattern.

05:12.400 --> 05:15.433
Plus, if our x is now x squared,

05:15.433 --> 05:18.100
x squared to the fourth
power is x to the eighth,

05:18.874 --> 05:22.400
x to the eighth power over four factorial,

05:23.233 --> 05:26.800
minus x squared to the sixth power

05:26.800 --> 05:31.533
is x to the 12th over six factorial,

05:32.167 --> 05:34.933
and then plus x squared to the eighth

05:34.933 --> 05:38.268
is x to the 16th power
over eight factorial,

05:38.667 --> 05:41.004
and of course, we can keep
going on and on and on.

05:41.007 --> 05:43.433
Minus, plus. But we just care
about the first five terms,

05:43.433 --> 05:45.133
the non-zero terms, and we're just saying

05:45.133 --> 05:47.139
this is an approximation, anyway.

05:47.139 --> 05:49.333
So we can say that this is going to be

05:49.333 --> 05:50.535
approximately equal to,

05:50.535 --> 05:52.000
we distribute the x to the third,

05:52.000 --> 05:54.200
and I'm going to do it
in magenta just for fun,

05:54.200 --> 05:55.968
so distribute the x to the third,

05:55.968 --> 05:57.833
we get x to the third,

05:58.435 --> 06:03.435
minus x to the seventh over two factorial,

06:04.166 --> 06:09.166
plus x to the 11th over four factorial,

06:10.001 --> 06:14.733
minus x to the 15th over six factorial,

06:15.400 --> 06:18.674
plus x to the 19th ...

06:19.167 --> 06:23.433
x to the 19th over eight factorial.

06:23.433 --> 06:25.133
That's the first five non-zero terms,

06:25.133 --> 06:26.633
and we are done.

06:26.633 --> 06:28.666
When you actually see what we got,

06:28.666 --> 06:32.233
you realize it would have
taken you forever to do this

06:32.233 --> 06:35.467
by, I guess you could say, by brute force,

06:35.467 --> 06:38.833
because you would have had
to find the 19th derivative

06:38.833 --> 06:40.169
of all of this madness.

06:40.169 --> 06:41.533
But when you realize that, "Hey, gee,

06:41.533 --> 06:43.733
"if I can just re-express this function

06:43.733 --> 06:45.967
"as, essentially,

06:47.490 --> 06:49.767
"x to a power times

06:50.733 --> 06:53.066
"something that I know
the Maclaurin series of,

06:53.066 --> 06:53.833
"especially ..."

06:53.833 --> 06:55.067
Well, if you view it this way:

06:55.067 --> 06:58.000
if I can write, if I can rewrite ...

06:58.000 --> 06:59.800
Let me say it in a less confusing way.

07:00.333 --> 07:02.267
If I can rewrite my function

07:02.267 --> 07:04.967
so it is equal to some ...

07:04.967 --> 07:06.400
Actually, I can even
throw a coefficient here.

07:06.400 --> 07:10.266
If it's equal to some A x to the n power,

07:10.673 --> 07:12.800
times some function ...

07:13.166 --> 07:14.100
Let me just use a new color.

07:14.100 --> 07:15.933
Times, purple.

07:16.500 --> 07:21.500
Times g of B,

07:22.766 --> 07:25.200
B x to some other power,

07:26.036 --> 07:28.534
where I can fairly easily,

07:28.534 --> 07:29.667
without too much computation,

07:29.667 --> 07:30.804
maybe I already know,

07:30.804 --> 07:34.534
if I know the Maclaurin series
representation of g of x,

07:34.867 --> 07:36.600
if I know what g of x is going to be,

07:36.600 --> 07:38.800
then I can do exactly what
I did just in this video.

07:38.800 --> 07:41.233
I find the Maclaurin series
representation for g,

07:41.233 --> 07:42.266
every place where I saw an x,

07:42.266 --> 07:44.366
I replaced it with what I have over here,

07:44.366 --> 07:47.633
the B x to the m power,
where m is some exponent.

07:47.933 --> 07:49.567
That will give me another polynomial,

07:49.567 --> 07:50.833
another power series,

07:50.833 --> 07:52.900
and then I multiply it time A x to the n,

07:52.900 --> 07:54.033
and that's going to, once again,

07:54.033 --> 07:54.733
give me another power series,

07:54.733 --> 07:57.702
and that will be the power
series for my original function.

07:58.133 --> 07:59.333
Very exciting.
